---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { commitHash } from "../lib/commit";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Colophon — how this site is made · burningsuit"
  description="How this site is made: the type, the stack, the strict CSP, and why there are no trackers. The site is the work sample."
>
  <Fragment slot="footer-invitation"
    >If you're the engineer who'll have to live with what I build, this page is
    for you. Ask me anything about how it's put together</Fragment
  >

  <section class="page-hero" data-ground="aubergine">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker meta enter">colophon</p>
      <h1 class="enter appear-300">how this <em>site</em> is made.</h1>
      <p class="lead enter appear-450">
        I build the things I advise on, so it seems only fair to show my working.
        This site is small on purpose: it should load instantly on a phone, on
        a train, on cellular — and it should still be readable if everything
        clever fails to load.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="thread" data-reveal-raw aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <section class="chapter" data-ground="aubergine" aria-labelledby="type">
    <div class="wrap what">
      <h2 class="label kicker meta" id="type" data-reveal>the type</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p data-reveal>
          Three roles. <strong>Mona Sans</strong> for headings, nav and the big
          moments — GitHub's grotesque, with enough engineering in it to feel
          technical and enough character to feel warm. <strong>Albert Sans</strong>
          for body, a warm humanist sans standing in for the brand's Bilo. And
          <strong>Commit Mono</strong> carries the small voice — the asides, the
          labels, the clock, this line. Emphasis is a shift in colour, not a
          change of typeface; there's no serif, and nothing leans into italic.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          Every face is subset to the Latin characters the site actually uses,
          served as woff2 from this domain, and preloaded — no third-party font
          CDN, no flash of invisible text, no request leaving for Google on your
          behalf.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" data-ground="aubergine" aria-labelledby="stack">
    <div class="wrap what">
      <h2 class="label kicker meta" id="stack" data-reveal>the stack</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p data-reveal>
          <strong>Astro</strong> builds it to static HTML; <strong>Tailwind</strong>
          configures the design system in CSS. There is no server, no database,
          no client framework hydrating in the background — just files, served
          from <strong>GitHub Pages</strong> over a custom domain. Images are
          generated at build time as AVIF with a WebP fallback.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          The motion is hand-written CSS on a tight budget: two speeds, three
          easings, and a system that respects <em>reduced motion</em> as a real,
          designed alternative rather than an afterthought. Nothing on the page
          waits for JavaScript to become readable.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" data-ground="aubergine" aria-labelledby="quiet">
    <div class="wrap what">
      <h2 class="label kicker meta" id="quiet" data-reveal>what it doesn't do</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p data-reveal>
          No analytics. No cookies. No consent banner, because there's nothing to
          consent to. No third-party scripts, no tracking pixels, no fonts phoning
          home. I don't know that you're reading this, and I don't need to.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          A strict Content Security Policy backs that up: by default the page is
          allowed to load <em>nothing</em>, and then permitted, one line at a
          time, only the things it genuinely needs — its own styles, its own
          scripts, its own fonts, and images from itself. It ships as a
          <code class="meta">&lt;meta&gt;</code> tag because GitHub Pages can't
          send response headers; the one trade-off that costs is noted in the
          source.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" data-ground="aubergine" aria-labelledby="sample">
    <div class="wrap what">
      <h2 class="label kicker meta" id="sample" data-reveal>the site is the sample</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p data-reveal>
          A lot of what I do is help teams tell capable from trustworthy. This
          site is a small argument that I hold myself to the same line: it's
          accessible, fast, and honest about its own internals. If it isn't, tell
          me — that's a fair thing to hold me to.
        </p>
        <p class="meta" data-reveal>
          this build: <span>{commitHash}</span> · west sussex, england{
            /* No id here: the Footer (rendered on every page) already carries
               id="commit", so a second one collided on /colophon. This span is
               purely decorative — nothing scripts it — so it just drops the id. */
          }
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="thread thread-end" data-reveal-raw aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="talk-spacer" data-ground="aubergine"></div>
</BaseLayout>
